TI  - Living Shorelines Equal or Outperform Natural Shorelines as Fish Habitat Over Time: Updated Results from a Long-Term BACI Study at Multiple Sites
T2  - ESTUARIES AND COASTS
AB  - There is increasing global interest in enhancing coastal resilience to hazards like hurricanes, floods, and sea level rise by using ecological engineering techniques. Living shorelines, a type of ecologically engineered coastal protection, are often hailed as a potential win-win solution that can provide vital coastal protection services while also benefiting biodiversity and the delivery of various ecosystem services; however, there is limited rigorous experimental data on the effects of living shorelines on the nekton community over longer time frames. Here, we use a Before-After-Control-Impact (BACI) Paired Series design at four separate living shorelines in North Carolina, USA, to better understand how nekton biomass, abundance, richness, and community composition changed from before living shoreline construction to 4-5 years after construction. We found that nekton metrics were similar at living shoreline and natural reference sites, with enhancement of nekton abundance, biomass, or richness observed at two of the four sites. Several response metrics increased linearly over time (rather than immediately post-construction), suggesting that the benefits of living shorelines to nekton can take time to develop. Additionally, contrary to our hypothesis, enhancement was only observed at the two sites with granite rock sills, and not at the sites with oyster bag sills, which may relate to the structural stability of the sills. Altogether, our results suggest that 4-5 years post-restoration, there were no negative effects of living shoreline installation on nekton, and in several cases, there were benefits, but more research is needed to fully understand the ecological effects of material choice on nekton use of living shorelines.
