AU  - Li, JJ
AU  - Wang, J
AU  - Taning, CNT
AU  - De Schutter, K
TI  - Genetic engineering and genetic control in Coleoptera: progress, challenges and perspectives
T2  - ENTOMOLOGIA GENERALIS
AB  - Coleoptera, the largest order of insects, plays vital roles in ecosystems but also poses major threats to agriculture and forestry. While integrated pest management (IPM) has helped mitigate some of these impacts, the reliance on chemical pesticides and the rise of resistant pests highlights the urgent need for sustainable alternatives. Genetic control strategies offer an environmentally friendly and targeted approach to managing pest populations. Early tools, such as transposonbased systems, laid the foundation for genetic transformations, while later advances like zinc finger nucleases (ZFNs) and transcription activator-like effector nucleases (TALENs) enhanced precision by integrating a genetic cassette at the targeted site. The introduction of the CRISPR/Cas9 system has since revolutionized beetle research, offering a relatively simple, e1I cient, and cost-effective tool for genetic studies and pest control applications. Despite its great potential, the application of CRISPR/Cas9-based genome editing in beetles has progressed slower compared to other insect orders such as Diptera and Lepidoptera. Challenges include the limited availability of species-specific tools, ine1I ciencies in delivering the molecular components, and concerns about off-target effects. CRISPR-based strategies, such as gene drives and precision-guided sterile insect techniques (pgSIT), hold great promise but raise biosecurity concerns, including ecological risks and unintended consequences. This review emphasizes the importance of advancing genetic engineering technologies in beetles while fostering the development of regulatory frameworks and encouraging public engagement to ensure sustainable and responsible pest management solutions.
