TI  - Silica-structured lignin biochar engineered via pyrolysis for multifunctional remediation of Cd-contaminated soils
T2  - JOURNAL OF CLEANER PRODUCTION
AB  - Soil cadmium (Cd) pollution poses a serious threat to food security and ecosystem health. Silicon (Si)-modified biochar shows great promise for remediation; however, the critical influence of the timing of Si introduction (whether during hydrothermal or pyrolysis synthesis) on Si speciation, stability, and interactions within soil-plant systems has been largely overlooked. This study systematically compares two preparation methods: introducing silicon during hydrothermal (HPC-Si@HT) or pyrolysis (HPC-Si@PY) stages. Comprehensive characterization revealed that HPC-Si@PY formed a stable amorphous silica structure, yielding a significantly higher Cd2+ adsorption capacity (315.53 mg/g) than HPC-Si@HT, primarily via ion exchange and C-Si complexation. In contrast, HPC-Si@HT, with lower stability, relied on complexation and precipitation. Pot experiments confirmed the remarkable remediation efficacy of HPC-Si@PY: it reduced exchangeable Cd by 26.1 % while increasing residual Cd by 143.3 % through silicon-driven precipitation and pH modulation. Furthermore, HPC-Si@PY enhances soil multifunctionality by improving nutrient cycling and enriching Cd-resistant microbes. For Pakchoi, HPC-Si@PY increases biomass by 81.67 % and reduces Cd accumulation by 68.96 % through integrated mechanisms of rhizosphere immobilization, antioxidant activation, and silicon-mediated deposition. The limited performance of HPC-Si@HT underscores the fundamental importance of incorporating silicon during the pyrolysis stage to create stable, reactive amorphous silica structures. These findings establish crucial design principles for engineering effective silicon-biochar that enable superior cross-scale remediation of Cdcontaminated environments.
