AU  - Chen, RS
TI  - Fifteen years of grazing exclusion reshapes vegetation structure and landscape connectivity across alpine ecosystems in the Qilian Mountain, Northwestern China
T2  - JOURNAL OF ENVIRONMENTAL MANAGEMENT
AB  - High-altitude and high-latitude ecosystems are among the most vulnerable to climate change and human disturbance, with widespread degradation threatening their role in water regulation, biodiversity conservation, and carbon sequestration. Livestock-exclusion enclosure is widely applied for alpine restoration, yet its ecological outcomes remain poorly understood across elevation gradients and ecosystem types. To address this, a 15-year grazing-exclusion experiment was conducted in a vertical transect spanning 2980-4164 m a.s.l. in the Qilian Mountains, where paired fence-enclosed plots (EN) and adjacent grazed controls (CK) were both settled in each site with automated meteorological sensors equipped. The results showed that long-term enclosure significantly increased both above ground biomass (AGB) and litter biomass (LB), with the greatest gains in shrub meadows (AGB+2.2-fold, LB+8.8-fold) and swampy meadows (AGB+2.6-fold, LB+2.8-fold). The EN plots also reduced landscape fragmentation and enhanced patch connectivity, with the percentage of landscape (PLAND) of shrub and swampy meadows increased by 3.1 % and 5.1 %, respectively, while the largest patch index (LPI) rose by 3.1 % and 16.2 %, respectively. However, it led to community homogenization, reducing species heterogeneity and favoring sedge dominance while facilitating grass establishment. Despite this simplification, forbs and legumes were maintained under fencing, indicating functional group resilience. These findings suggest that grazing exclusion is most beneficial in moisture-rich ecosystems at mid-elevation zones, where it maximizes vegetation recovery and landscape cohesion. Our study provides elevation-specific evidence to guide targeted restoration strategies, and highlights the importance of integrating vegetation structure, diversity, and spatial pattern indicators in long-term alpine ecosystem management.
